    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `format_for_display` in `app/report.py`, and all 3 tests pass (the 2 that were already there plus 1 I added).\n\n**How it works:** it uses `DataFrame.map` to format each cell and returns a new table, so the input table isn't changed. The index and columns stay the same. For each cell:\n- missing \u2192 `\"\"`\n- zero \u2192 `\"-\"`\n- positive \u2192 `\"12,500.00\"`\n- negative \u2192 `\"(634.20)\"`\n\n**One choice to check:** I round each amount to two decimals before checking for zero. Otherwise, tiny leftovers from adding up floats (e.g. `1e-12`) or amounts under half a cent (e.g. `-0.001`) would show as `\"0.00\"` or `\"(0.00)\"`. With the rounding they show as `\"-\"`, which I assumed is what finance wants to see.\n\n**Test:** I added `tests/test_report.py`. It checks all four cases, the rounding behaviour above, that the index and columns match, and that the input table is unchanged.\n\nNo dependencies were added. Following the project notes, I looked up the pandas docs for `DataFrame.map` and `pd.isna` before writing the code."
